WEBVTT

00:00.240 --> 00:06.140
Usually, when we talk about a medical diagnosis, you know, there is this underlying expectation of precision.

00:06.520 --> 00:07.700
Right. Yeah, total precision.

00:08.040 --> 00:14.220
Like, you break your arm, the x-ray reveals this jagged white line right through the radius, and the doctor simply points to it.

00:14.640 --> 00:15.900
Broken or not broken.

00:16.060 --> 00:16.580
It's binary.

00:16.900 --> 00:19.500
Exactly. It is a binary objective reality.

00:19.920 --> 00:27.900
And that brings, I think, a strange kind of comfort, because we desperately want the world to operate as this neat, categorical filing cabinet of facts.

00:27.900 --> 00:29.600
We really do. We crave that certainty.

00:30.000 --> 00:39.800
But the moment you step into the world of artificial intelligence, specifically into the architecture of large language models that are processing human knowledge, that x-ray machine just completely shatters.

00:40.080 --> 00:41.400
It shatters into a million pieces.

00:41.540 --> 00:41.700
Yeah.

00:41.960 --> 00:48.280
Because the diagnostic landscape in artificial cognition, it just doesn't offer us clean fractures.

00:48.380 --> 00:48.960
No, it doesn't.

00:48.960 --> 00:59.980
We are looking at systems where the boundary between genuine comprehension and the, you know, flawless statistical mimicry of comprehension is just entirely porous.

01:00.120 --> 01:01.080
It's all blurred together.

01:01.500 --> 01:04.840
Which brings us directly to the medical file we are examining today for you.

01:05.140 --> 01:08.720
We are tracing the life cycle of a single, highly obscure word.

01:08.720 --> 01:10.860
A very, very obscure word.

01:10.940 --> 01:15.760
Right. A word that appeared exactly once in the text of a 1984 philosophy book.

01:15.880 --> 01:21.300
Then it essentially died out and was then bizarrely resurrected by modern technology.

01:21.480 --> 01:22.980
Like a linguistic zombie.

01:23.260 --> 01:31.460
Yes. And this deep dive today is based on a staggering independent research paper from October 2026 by an author named Flixion.

01:31.460 --> 01:32.620
It's a brilliant paper.

01:32.820 --> 01:39.640
It really is. And the reason this matters to you, the reason you should care about some forgotten philosophical term from 40 years ago,

01:39.740 --> 01:46.360
is because the very AI assistants you probably used this morning, you know, to summarize your emails or search the web.

01:46.540 --> 01:47.920
Or write a grocery list.

01:48.120 --> 01:53.600
Exactly. They are actively falling into the exact cognitive trap this word was invented to describe.

01:53.820 --> 01:54.980
They're walking right into it.

01:54.980 --> 02:01.780
We are looking at the hidden structural cost that our brains and our machines pay to process information quickly.

02:02.160 --> 02:05.520
So we're going to play linguistic detective first to find the true meaning.

02:05.880 --> 02:08.600
Then look at how modern AI completely botched it.

02:08.900 --> 02:12.860
And finally explore a cutting edge geometric model mapping this whole mess out.

02:13.400 --> 02:14.220
Sounds like quite the journey.

02:14.700 --> 02:16.120
Okay. Let's unpack this.

02:16.340 --> 02:18.480
We have to start with the artifact itself.

02:18.800 --> 02:19.840
Frozen in 1984.

02:20.420 --> 02:21.400
What is this word?

02:22.020 --> 02:24.500
So the word is syntactitude.

02:25.100 --> 02:25.780
Syntactitude.

02:25.960 --> 02:26.080
Yeah.

02:26.240 --> 02:26.360
Yeah.

02:26.660 --> 02:33.360
And it exists on page 31 of Daniel Dennett's book, Elbow Room, The Varieties of Free Will Worth Wanting.

02:33.820 --> 02:34.900
Page 31.

02:35.520 --> 02:36.560
Just page 31.

02:37.200 --> 02:41.280
It sits inside a chapter titled, Making Reason Practical.

02:41.820 --> 02:47.120
And its existence really highlights the terrifying fragility of specialized knowledge.

02:47.540 --> 02:48.000
How so?

02:48.000 --> 02:57.480
Well, when a term survives in only a single sentence of a single book, its conceptual half-life is incredibly short.

02:57.480 --> 03:04.700
I mean, the broad atmosphere of Dennett's arguments survives, but the surgical precision of the definition melts.

03:05.380 --> 03:09.660
It melts into whatever neighboring concept the culture finds easiest to remember.

03:09.660 --> 03:13.080
So it's like playing a game of telephone over four decades.

03:13.680 --> 03:14.040
Precisely.

03:14.680 --> 03:20.900
So to salvage the original definition before it degrades, we really have to understand Dennett's framework first.

03:21.020 --> 03:21.180
Okay.

03:21.300 --> 03:25.280
So he proposes that we can describe the human brain through two very different lenses.

03:25.860 --> 03:26.300
Right.

03:26.460 --> 03:29.740
The semantic engine and the syntactic engine.

03:30.200 --> 03:31.280
Let's break those down.

03:31.420 --> 03:32.120
Let's break those down.

03:32.120 --> 03:33.700
What is the first lens?

03:34.120 --> 03:39.460
So in the first lens, a semantic engine is a pure manipulator of meaning.

03:39.460 --> 03:48.160
It is a system that evaluates the intrinsic significance, the truth value, and the deep reference of the information it receives.

03:48.920 --> 03:51.520
It actually understands what it's looking at.

03:51.740 --> 03:52.180
Exactly.

03:52.400 --> 03:52.520
Yeah.

03:52.880 --> 03:56.160
A true semantic engine cares about the why.

03:56.520 --> 03:57.860
It cares about meaning.

03:58.160 --> 04:00.960
But physical reality doesn't operate on meaning, right?

04:00.960 --> 04:03.920
It operates on physics, chemistry, biology.

04:04.540 --> 04:06.400
And that is the core of the second lens.

04:06.820 --> 04:09.700
From a physical standpoint, the brain is just an organ.

04:10.120 --> 04:10.980
Just a lump of tissue.

04:11.280 --> 04:11.480
Right.

04:11.820 --> 04:13.780
It is a syntactic engine.

04:14.340 --> 04:20.440
It's a mechanistic system responding exclusively to the structural, formal properties of its inputs.

04:21.020 --> 04:26.980
The shape of the sensory data, the electrochemical impulses, the hard-coded rules of biological circuitry.

04:27.340 --> 04:28.940
It's just following a script.

04:28.940 --> 04:33.160
A syntactic engine does not know what it is processing.

04:33.380 --> 04:36.660
It simply executes the physical rules it was built with.

04:37.280 --> 04:37.420
Okay.

04:37.500 --> 04:40.280
Think about a highly automated mail sorting facility.

04:40.520 --> 04:41.620
I think this analogy helps.

04:41.760 --> 04:42.500
I like this analogy.

04:42.640 --> 04:48.180
A purely syntactic engine is like the optical scanner that looks exclusively at the zip code on a letter.

04:48.480 --> 04:49.200
Just the numbers.

04:49.460 --> 04:49.640
Right.

04:49.640 --> 04:54.900
It reads 90210, it triggers a physical gate, and the letter drops into the California bin.

04:55.100 --> 04:59.700
It reads 10001, the gate shifts, the letter drops into the New York bin.

05:00.080 --> 05:00.880
It's flawless.

05:01.560 --> 05:06.580
The machine operates flawlessly, based purely on the physical geometry of the ink on the envelope.

05:06.580 --> 05:11.020
But it possesses zero conceptual awareness of what New York actually is.

05:11.480 --> 05:12.500
It doesn't know about pizza.

05:13.300 --> 05:13.940
Or traffic.

05:14.320 --> 05:14.480
No.

05:14.880 --> 05:19.000
It doesn't know about the Empire State Building, or the subway system, or the whole concept of a city.

05:19.180 --> 05:22.740
It just processes syntax, the shape of the numbers.

05:23.020 --> 05:25.780
And the syntax does not determine the semantics.

05:26.240 --> 05:28.660
The zip code doesn't explain the letter's emotional weight.

05:28.860 --> 05:31.460
It could be a tax bill or a love letter.

05:31.860 --> 05:32.880
The machine doesn't care.

05:32.880 --> 05:39.060
Dennett argues that because of this disconnect, a perfect semantic engine is a physical impossibility.

05:39.500 --> 05:42.840
He compares it to the perpetual motion machine of cognitive science.

05:43.240 --> 05:45.960
It just can't exist in the real physical world.

05:46.320 --> 05:50.180
He relates it to the perfect Kantian rational will, right?

05:50.440 --> 05:51.240
Yes, exactly.

05:51.660 --> 05:58.660
The idea of a completely friction-free intellect that is infinitely and perfectly alert to every subtle nuance of truth.

05:58.660 --> 06:02.180
Yeah, the Kantian ideal is this beautiful abstraction.

06:02.180 --> 06:04.880
But you cannot construct it out of neurons.

06:05.400 --> 06:07.520
And you cannot construct it out of silicon, either.

06:08.060 --> 06:10.080
Because physical systems have limits.

06:10.500 --> 06:10.720
Right.

06:10.960 --> 06:15.520
They have finite processing power, they have finite memory, and they have finite time.

06:16.300 --> 06:23.180
A perfect semantic engine requires infinite resources to fully evaluate the absolute truth of every single input.

06:23.620 --> 06:25.180
Which we obviously don't have.

06:25.520 --> 06:27.840
So evolution had to compromise somewhere.

06:28.520 --> 06:31.520
If we can't be perfect semantic engines, what are we?

06:31.520 --> 06:34.900
We are very good physical substitutes.

06:35.520 --> 06:45.700
We are syntactic engines, rule-following biological machines that have been cunningly sculpted by natural selection to approximate the behavior of a semantic engine.

06:45.920 --> 06:47.880
We fake it till we make it.

06:48.360 --> 06:49.120
Kinda, yeah.

06:49.120 --> 06:54.160
We aren't infinitely sensitive to meaning, but we are indefinitely sensitive.

06:54.880 --> 07:03.220
We mimic semantic understanding so well that we can navigate complex environments, but we do it using physical syntactic shortcuts.

07:03.220 --> 07:09.400
And Dennett claims these shortcuts make us practically invulnerable to something called sfexiousness.

07:09.860 --> 07:11.400
Yes, sfexiousness.

07:11.780 --> 07:15.280
Which the paper notes he borrowed from the cognitive scientist Douglas Hofstadter.

07:15.640 --> 07:16.060
That's right.

07:16.840 --> 07:20.100
And sfexiousness is just a brilliant concept.

07:20.600 --> 07:26.160
It's derived from the actual, documented behavior of the digger wasp, genus Sfex.

07:26.660 --> 07:27.620
Tell us about the wasp.

07:27.620 --> 07:31.900
So, the wasp exhibits this notoriously rigid behavioral loop.

07:32.560 --> 07:43.720
When it brings a paralyzed cricket back to its burrow to feed its larva, it leaves the cricket on the threshold, goes inside to inspect the burrow for predators, and then emerges to drag the cricket down.

07:44.100 --> 07:47.980
Which, on the surface, is a fantastic survival mechanism, checking for danger.

07:47.980 --> 07:52.820
It is. Absolutely. Provided the environment remains perfectly static.

07:53.360 --> 08:01.360
But, uh, if a researcher intervenes and moves the cricket just a few inches away while the wasp is inside the burrow, the routine breaks.

08:01.900 --> 08:02.420
What happens?

08:02.840 --> 08:10.320
The wasp emerges, finds the cricket displaced, drags it back to the threshold, and then goes back inside to inspect the burrow again.

08:10.700 --> 08:12.580
Even though it literally just checked it.

08:12.580 --> 08:20.140
Exactly. The researcher can move the cricket 40 times, and the wasp will repeat the inspection loop 40 times.

08:20.820 --> 08:26.360
It cannot conceptualize the broader semantic reality that the burrow was already checked.

08:26.580 --> 08:27.560
It's trapped.

08:28.000 --> 08:30.080
It is a prisoner of its own syntax.

08:30.700 --> 08:34.300
Its rule is just drop cricket, check hole, pull cricket.

08:34.680 --> 08:37.300
If step one is reset, the whole program resets.

08:37.600 --> 08:39.160
It is a biological Roomba.

08:39.700 --> 08:41.500
A biological Roomba, yes.

08:41.500 --> 08:46.780
Like an early model robotic vacuum getting trapped in a corner, continuously bumping into the same chair leg,

08:46.840 --> 08:53.280
because its hard-coded syntactic rule is simply, if bump, back up, rotate five degrees, move forward.

08:53.440 --> 08:57.720
Right. It lacks any spatial semantic map of the room. It has no idea it's in a corner.

08:58.320 --> 09:02.360
A perfect semantic engine would instantly realize the absurdity of the loop.

09:02.420 --> 09:06.080
It would say, I've hit this chair four times. I need to go the other way.

09:06.080 --> 09:12.140
But human cognition escapes this fexish loop, mostly through reflexivity.

09:13.000 --> 09:18.780
Hofstetter and Dennett point out that human intelligence explodes when pattern recognition turns inward.

09:19.440 --> 09:21.560
We monitor our own representations.

09:22.280 --> 09:23.300
We watch ourselves thinking.

09:23.300 --> 09:29.320
Yes. If you find yourself checking the refrigerator three times in ten minutes and finding nothing,

09:29.760 --> 09:31.900
your brain eventually flags the repetition.

09:32.560 --> 09:35.900
You step outside the loop and say, I am acting irrationally.

09:36.200 --> 09:37.640
Food hasn't magically appeared.

09:38.160 --> 09:40.160
We run diagnostics on our own syntax.

09:40.680 --> 09:41.060
We do.

09:41.060 --> 09:46.420
But, and this brings us to the exact definition on page 31 of Elbow Room,

09:47.140 --> 09:51.100
that self-monitoring capacity has a hard mathematical limit.

09:52.040 --> 09:52.640
Because we're physical.

09:53.140 --> 09:57.520
Right. You cannot reflect on all of your cognitive activity simultaneously.

09:58.040 --> 09:59.900
We hit the wall of physical finitude.

10:00.440 --> 10:05.160
Exactly. Dennett invokes the halting problem from computer science to prove this.

10:05.460 --> 10:06.520
Turing's halting problem.

10:06.520 --> 10:15.380
Yeah. Just as Alan Turing proved that no algorithm can perfectly determine whether every possible program will eventually halt or run forever,

10:16.020 --> 10:22.980
no finite cognitive system can perfectly monitor its own behavior without eventually running out of computational runway.

10:23.500 --> 10:24.880
You just run out of metal levels.

10:25.440 --> 10:28.580
Right. Or, more practically, you just run out of time.

10:28.780 --> 10:33.440
So a blind spot is structurally guaranteed. It's not a mistake. It's baked in.

10:33.920 --> 10:34.900
It is unavoidable.

10:34.900 --> 10:43.220
Any system attempting to approximate infinite semantic perfection using finite syntactic hardware will leave a residue.

10:43.980 --> 10:45.160
A leftover vulnerability.

10:45.840 --> 10:51.280
If you place a human in an environment that is sufficiently complex or put them under enough temporal pressure,

10:51.660 --> 10:55.700
you will eventually expose their normally invisible irrationality.

10:56.120 --> 10:57.800
They'll do something totally spectious.

10:58.400 --> 11:03.780
Right. And that residual hardwired vulnerability, that is their syntactitude.

11:03.780 --> 11:06.960
Wow. Okay, that is syntactitude.

11:07.280 --> 11:10.000
That is the pristine 1984 definition.

11:10.560 --> 11:13.520
Syntactitude is not the state of being a syntactic engine.

11:14.060 --> 11:18.380
Syntactitude is the specific unavoidable residue of irrationality.

11:18.880 --> 11:28.220
It's the lingering vulnerability to spexic ruts that results from a finite syntactic engine attempting to perform as a perfect semantic engine.

11:28.220 --> 11:34.940
But the paper highlights a crucial footnote here, where Dennett completely reframes how we should actually view this vulnerability.

11:35.760 --> 11:38.760
Syntactitude isn't just a bug in the human operating system.

11:39.080 --> 11:42.940
It is a feature. It's the necessary cost of doing business in real time.

11:43.720 --> 11:45.120
That footnote is vital.

11:45.720 --> 11:53.460
Dennett writes that human ruts are often recognizable as our amiable quirks, our artistic styles, or our cognitive heuristics.

11:53.900 --> 11:54.480
Our shortcuts.

11:54.480 --> 12:02.420
Exactly. Syntactitude might cause us to blindly follow a bad habit, but it is the economic tradeoff we make for speed.

12:02.880 --> 12:11.220
If we actually tried to perfectly evaluate the deep semantic truth of every shadow moving in the grass, we would be paralyzed by computation.

12:11.920 --> 12:14.780
We'd get eaten by the tiger while trying to calculate the meaning of the shadow.

12:15.120 --> 12:18.400
Precisely. Syntactitude is the price we pay for action.

12:18.400 --> 12:22.320
We trade ultimate accuracy for survival speed. That makes so much sense.

12:22.840 --> 12:27.260
But, I mean, that is a highly specific, complex, and surgical concept.

12:27.600 --> 12:28.180
Very surgical.

12:28.580 --> 12:33.360
But words left unattended in academia for 40 years tend to mutate.

12:33.660 --> 12:37.860
So if the definition is so specific, what happened to it over the last 40 years?

12:37.960 --> 12:40.200
How exactly did this definition erode?

12:40.200 --> 12:45.020
Well, the paper acts as this amazing forensic analysis of semantic drift.

12:45.540 --> 12:51.340
It identifies two distinct, highly tempting substitutions that scholars made over the decades as they cited Dennett.

12:51.580 --> 12:52.380
What's the first one?

12:52.700 --> 12:58.220
The first substitution simply equates syntactitude with the state of being a syntactic engine.

12:58.600 --> 13:00.460
Which flattens the concept entirely.

13:00.860 --> 13:01.500
It really does.

13:01.980 --> 13:07.400
Dennett describes syntactic engines on page 28, but syntactitude doesn't appear until page 31.

13:07.400 --> 13:16.740
Any computer is a syntactic engine, but syntactitude is the performance glitch of a system trying to transcend its own hardware limitations.

13:17.340 --> 13:25.000
Confusing the two is like conflating the internal combustion engine of a car with the specific phenomenon of the engine stalling at high altitudes.

13:25.380 --> 13:26.420
That's a great way to put it.

13:26.800 --> 13:33.160
They are related, obviously, but one is the mechanism, and the other is the boundary condition of that mechanism's performance.

13:33.560 --> 13:36.280
Right. What's the second substitution the paper found?

13:36.280 --> 13:39.380
The second substitution is even further afield.

13:39.920 --> 13:44.060
People began using syntactitude as a shorthand for amoral grammar.

13:44.480 --> 13:45.460
Amoral grammar.

13:45.700 --> 13:45.900
Yeah.

13:46.380 --> 13:54.440
The idea that a sentence can be perfectly structured according to the rules of syntax, yet conveys something entirely false or ethically abhorrent.

13:54.840 --> 14:01.140
I mean, while that's true, that has absolutely nothing to do with the physical limits of self-monitoring in cognitive systems.

14:01.140 --> 14:02.100
Nothing at all.

14:02.460 --> 14:10.620
But if we connect this to the bigger picture, the paper tracks this degradation through academic citations over the years, and you can see it happening.

14:11.180 --> 14:21.700
For instance, in 1990, a scholar named Mark Leon published a paper using syntactitude to describe the existential fear that our rationality is devoid of actual meaning.

14:21.700 --> 14:25.040
The anxiety that we are nothing but mechanical engines.

14:25.420 --> 14:25.520
Right.

14:26.060 --> 14:32.860
So the concept shifted from a computationally precise tradeoff into this generalized philosophical dread.

14:33.280 --> 14:33.640
Exactly.

14:34.240 --> 14:35.540
And the drift continues.

14:36.100 --> 14:38.940
In 2009, a writer named Schollmeyer uses the term.

14:38.940 --> 14:46.720
Now, he correctly aligns with Dennett that we are subject to syntactitude, but he restricts the cause entirely to a lack of time.

14:47.220 --> 14:50.840
Instead of a fundamental imperfection in our rationality's architecture.

14:51.180 --> 14:51.480
Yes.

14:51.980 --> 14:54.940
He misses the deep structural limit Dennett was pointing out.

14:55.300 --> 14:59.660
And then, this is my favorite part, the paper uncovers a ghost citation.

15:00.220 --> 15:02.160
The ghost citation is fascinating.

15:02.160 --> 15:11.840
In 2012, in the reference list of a master's thesis by Andrew Nicholson, there is an entry for an unpublished manuscript simply titled The Problem of Syntactitude.

15:12.280 --> 15:12.520
Yeah.

15:12.640 --> 15:21.920
The author of our paper couldn't retrieve the manuscript itself because it was unpublished, but its mere existence in a thesis about mental content proves that the word had morphed.

15:21.960 --> 15:24.960
It had become a generalized, free-floating problem.

15:24.960 --> 15:36.140
The sharp edges of Dennett's original 1984 computational insight had been sanded down into this vague atmospheric proxy for the anxieties surrounding minds and machines.

15:36.440 --> 15:38.160
It lost all its original geometry.

15:38.600 --> 15:41.520
Which perfectly sets the stage for October 2026.

15:41.960 --> 15:44.620
Because FlyOcean didn't just write a history paper.

15:45.200 --> 15:47.560
They ran a stress test on modern infrastructure.

15:47.560 --> 15:48.380
They did.

15:48.740 --> 15:55.080
What happens when you ask modern technology to define this heavily mutated, extremely delicate, drifting word?

15:55.600 --> 15:57.580
Well, the methodology was tightly controlled.

15:58.380 --> 16:04.560
The author queried the exact term syntactitude in quotation marks, and they specifically added the name Hofstadter.

16:04.820 --> 16:08.540
Because Dennett explicitly noted that Hofstadter suggested the term to him.

16:08.920 --> 16:09.160
Right.

16:09.420 --> 16:12.720
So, they plugged this into a modern, generative AI search system.

16:12.880 --> 16:17.420
And the AI overview that generated at the top of the search results completely collapsed.

16:17.560 --> 16:20.900
But the way it collapsed is what makes this paper so compelling.

16:21.480 --> 16:24.700
Let's look at the failures, because there are three distinct ones.

16:25.040 --> 16:25.240
Right.

16:25.780 --> 16:28.020
The first failure is a classic substitution.

16:28.720 --> 16:39.960
The AI confidently defines syntactitude as the state of being a purely syntactic mechanism, a system that processes symbols and rules without any inherent understanding of their actual meaning.

16:40.380 --> 16:43.240
It swallowed the first academic substitution whole.

16:43.240 --> 16:50.100
It grabbed the page 28 concept of a syntactic engine and just pasted it over the page 31 concept of syntactitude.

16:50.580 --> 16:51.100
Exactly.

16:51.460 --> 16:57.880
It utilized a statistical association to replace a precise boundary condition with a generic class description.

16:58.680 --> 16:59.100
Amazing.

16:59.880 --> 17:00.820
And the second failure?

17:01.220 --> 17:04.540
The second is a failure of attribution and hallucinated authority.

17:04.540 --> 17:10.400
The AI stated, while cognitive scientist Douglas Hofstadter did not coin the word syntactitude.

17:10.400 --> 17:15.400
I have to pause here because this specific phrasing is just insidious.

17:15.780 --> 17:16.120
It is.

17:16.260 --> 17:22.780
Because technically, Dennett says Hofstadter suggested it, meaning Dennett coined it in print.

17:23.080 --> 17:31.140
So, the AI statement that Hofstadter did not coin it isn't a grammatical lie, but it is an egregious failure of context.

17:31.520 --> 17:32.960
A massive omission.

17:32.960 --> 17:38.420
Dennett's book is literally the only primary source on Earth regarding the genesis of this word.

17:38.820 --> 17:48.140
For an AI to offer a definitive denial of Hofstadter's coinage, without ever mentioning that Hofstadter suggested it, creates a false aura of omniscient authority.

17:48.780 --> 17:57.520
Wait, if the AI says Hofstadter didn't coin it, and Dennett's book says he merely suggested it, isn't the AI technically right on a technicality?

17:57.900 --> 17:58.880
That's the danger.

17:58.880 --> 18:02.920
It is technically right in the most narrow, useless sense.

18:03.420 --> 18:05.980
This is a failure of sourcing and omission.

18:06.760 --> 18:12.220
A flat denial, without mentioning the only primary record that exists, is highly misleading to a learner.

18:12.720 --> 18:15.480
It makes it sound like Hofstadter had nothing to do with it.

18:15.840 --> 18:16.460
Which is false.

18:17.100 --> 18:21.180
And to compound the error, the AI generated a citation to support this claim, right?

18:21.320 --> 18:21.640
Yes.

18:21.640 --> 18:24.700
It pointed to a science fiction encyclopedia.

18:25.160 --> 18:28.140
A source entirely disconnected from the actual academic text.

18:28.540 --> 18:30.120
It just made up a fake link.

18:30.620 --> 18:31.520
Pure hallucination.

18:32.000 --> 18:34.660
But the final failure is really the masterpiece.

18:35.160 --> 18:36.060
The generalization.

18:36.220 --> 18:36.760
Let's hear it.

18:36.960 --> 18:46.360
The AI overview claimed that the word syntactitude addresses the philosophical anxiety at the heart of Hofstadter's work on artificial intelligence and consciousness.

18:46.360 --> 18:47.320
Wow.

18:47.820 --> 18:48.900
It sounds profound.

18:49.300 --> 18:50.580
It flows beautifully.

18:50.800 --> 18:51.220
It does.

18:51.500 --> 18:54.800
But it is entirely unfalsifiable fluff.

18:55.320 --> 19:04.020
It took the 1990 Mark Leon semantic drift, the idea that syntactitude means existential dread about machines, and grafted it onto Hofstadter.

19:04.020 --> 19:17.980
The AI ingested 40 years of academic telephone, mixed it with neighboring concepts, ignored the primary source material, fabricated a citation, and delivered the synthesis with flawless, unhesitating fluency.

19:18.380 --> 19:20.740
Here's where it gets really interesting.

19:21.380 --> 19:24.160
The meta joke of this experiment is breathtaking.

19:24.560 --> 19:25.220
It really is.

19:25.440 --> 19:28.680
The AI system is functioning as a statistical token predictor.

19:28.960 --> 19:30.740
It is a syntactic engine.

19:30.880 --> 19:31.140
Yes.

19:31.140 --> 19:37.840
When presented with the query, it is attempting to approximate semantic understanding to deliver a true definition.

19:38.620 --> 19:49.880
But due to its structural limitations, its reliance on associative proximity rather than grounded truth, it gets trapped in a highly plausible, deeply incorrect associative loop.

19:50.620 --> 19:53.580
The AI is literally exhibiting syntactitude.

19:54.040 --> 19:59.760
It is perfectly demonstrating the exact cognitive vulnerability it is currently failing to define.

19:59.760 --> 20:02.700
It is a syntactic engine caught in a rut.

20:03.260 --> 20:06.640
And the irony is weaponized by its own fluency.

20:07.160 --> 20:15.220
Because if a user reads that beautifully structured paragraph about philosophical anxiety, they have absolutely no reason to suspect it's a hallucination.

20:15.640 --> 20:16.000
None.

20:16.220 --> 20:18.920
The syntax masks the semantic void.

20:18.920 --> 20:25.920
Okay, so diagnosing that failure qualitatively is a lot of fun, but how do we actually measure or fix this?

20:26.060 --> 20:28.160
How do we map out getting it wrong?

20:28.480 --> 20:31.280
Because Flycone's paper pushes past just funny anecdotes.

20:31.780 --> 20:31.900
Right.

20:32.080 --> 20:41.480
It introduces a rigorous geometric model to map exactly how and why a system, whether it's biological or artificial, gets trapped in a fluent lie.

20:41.840 --> 20:43.440
Okay, let's get into the geometry.

20:43.440 --> 20:46.920
The paper proposes a geometric topology of meaning.

20:47.680 --> 20:54.900
Instead of treating a definition as a static entry in a dictionary, it models meaning as a space of all possible interpretations.

20:55.460 --> 20:57.880
A smooth, compact Romanian manifold.

20:58.560 --> 20:59.640
A Romanian manifold.

21:00.000 --> 21:01.620
Let's call this manifold M.

21:02.060 --> 21:06.380
I need to translate this math into a physical visualization to save you from math overload.

21:06.580 --> 21:07.020
Please do.

21:07.020 --> 21:14.920
Imagine this manifold M as a vast rolling landscape of hills and valleys, just a huge multidimensional terrain.

21:15.440 --> 21:19.860
Every single point on this landscape represents a different possible interpretation of a concept.

21:20.400 --> 21:27.360
Your brain's attempt to interpret something or an AI's attempt is like a ball rolling down these hills, pulled by gravity toward the lowest point.

21:27.360 --> 21:28.620
That's a perfect analogy.

21:29.260 --> 21:37.880
Because in a Riemannian manifold, the space is locally Euclidean, meaning we can measure distances and angles, and we can define smooth trajectories.

21:38.820 --> 21:40.440
So what dictates the movement of the ball?

21:40.540 --> 21:42.380
Why does it roll in one direction and not another?

21:42.520 --> 21:43.500
What is the gravity here?

21:43.940 --> 21:48.600
The gravity is a potential function, which the paper labels V subscript C.

21:49.120 --> 21:51.140
The subscript C stands for context.

21:51.780 --> 21:52.340
Context.

21:52.340 --> 21:59.060
Right. The specific prompt you give the AI, the surrounding text, the statistical weights of the neural network.

21:59.540 --> 22:04.620
All of this context generates a gradient. It creates the gravitational pull on the landscape.

22:05.040 --> 22:07.180
The system engages in gradient descent.

22:07.460 --> 22:13.320
The ball rolls down the slope, pulled by the contextual gravity, actively seeking to minimize its potential energy.

22:13.740 --> 22:15.640
It rolls until it finds a local minimum.

22:16.140 --> 22:16.520
A valley.

22:16.520 --> 22:25.800
Mathematically, this is a point where the gradient of the potential function is zero, and the Hessian matrix, which measures the curvature of the space, is positive definite.

22:26.360 --> 22:29.680
In planned terms, the ball settles at the bottom of a bowl and stops moving.

22:30.040 --> 22:30.360
Yes.

22:30.360 --> 22:35.100
The system reaches an equilibrium state and effectively declares, I have found the meaning.

22:35.600 --> 22:36.560
I am done processing.

22:37.340 --> 22:39.380
And the descent dynamics function flawlessly.

22:39.380 --> 22:44.280
But Flixian introduces a vital, independent constraint to this geometry.

22:45.240 --> 22:46.360
The acceptable set.

22:46.760 --> 22:47.860
The acceptable set.

22:48.340 --> 22:50.060
Let's label it RC.

22:50.580 --> 22:51.200
What is that?

22:51.940 --> 22:55.360
The acceptable set is the designated safe zone on the manifold.

22:56.160 --> 23:03.820
It represents the region of interpretations that are actually correct, factually grounded, and logically sound according to ground truth.

23:04.280 --> 23:07.140
So just finding a valley isn't enough.

23:07.140 --> 23:12.900
The system must find a valley that physically resides inside the boundaries of the acceptable set, RC.

23:13.600 --> 23:14.020
Exactly.

23:14.460 --> 23:17.600
Which brings us to the mathematical formalization of syntactitude.

23:18.320 --> 23:19.360
Tangential capture.

23:20.400 --> 23:21.560
Tangential capture.

23:22.000 --> 23:22.960
Okay, break that down.

23:23.480 --> 23:32.460
Tangential capture occurs when the contextual potential, VC, drives the state, the ball, into a strict local minimum, a deep, stable valley.

23:32.460 --> 23:37.680
But that valley is located strictly outside the acceptable set, RC.

23:38.500 --> 23:39.000
Oh, wow.

23:39.240 --> 23:42.600
So the system successfully completes its computational run.

23:42.780 --> 23:44.220
It feels completely stable.

23:44.420 --> 23:46.520
But the answer is fundamentally wrong.

23:47.020 --> 23:47.320
Yes.

23:47.900 --> 23:49.440
And notice what isn't happening.

23:50.020 --> 23:51.660
The AI hasn't broken down.

23:52.020 --> 23:56.900
It hasn't flown off the manifold into space, outputting chaotic noise or gibberish.

23:57.100 --> 23:59.960
It followed its own internal vector field perfectly.

23:59.960 --> 24:00.880
Right.

24:01.400 --> 24:05.720
It was captured by a tangent that felt stable, but was epistemically void.

24:06.400 --> 24:09.300
It ended up at an interpretation that reason would not accept.

24:10.120 --> 24:12.660
This maps perfectly onto the AI's hallucination about Dennett.

24:12.960 --> 24:21.620
The context of the words Hofstadter, syntactic mechanism, and philosophy warped the landscape, creating a very deep valley of associative fluff.

24:22.020 --> 24:27.240
The ball rolled down, settled into the philosophical anxiety definition, and just stopped.

24:27.240 --> 24:29.660
And the depth of that valley is critical.

24:30.180 --> 24:35.300
The paper connects this to the Hessian eigenvalue, which dictates the curvature of the local minimum.

24:36.200 --> 24:38.820
A larger eigenvalue means a steeper, deeper valley.

24:39.420 --> 24:42.600
When the valley is steep, the ball converges incredibly fast.

24:42.880 --> 24:43.220
Speed.

24:43.980 --> 24:47.080
The exact tradeoff Dennett highlighted in his 1984 footnote.

24:47.620 --> 24:49.440
We need fast convergence to survive.

24:50.080 --> 24:50.440
Exactly.

24:50.440 --> 24:57.020
If you're trying to interpret whether the sound in the brush is the wind or a predator, you need a steep valley.

24:57.500 --> 24:59.660
You need the ball to drop immediately so you can run.

25:00.140 --> 25:02.500
You can't spend 10 minutes rolling around shallow hills.

25:03.240 --> 25:05.240
Fast convergence produces fluency.

25:05.720 --> 25:07.000
It feels like certainty.

25:07.600 --> 25:10.000
But the mathematics show a dangerous disconnect.

25:10.000 --> 25:15.800
The speed of convergence depends entirely on the local geometry of the potential VC.

25:16.580 --> 25:23.340
But it is completely blind to whether that minimum lies inside the global constraint of the acceptable set RC.

25:24.360 --> 25:27.580
Settling alone supplies no certificate of correctness.

25:28.060 --> 25:29.100
Fluency is not truth.

25:29.960 --> 25:34.220
A confident AI is just an AI sitting in a deep geometric bowl.

25:34.220 --> 25:40.400
Okay, if the ball is stuck in the wrong valley, can't we just give it a little nudge?

25:40.700 --> 25:43.600
I mean, can't we just tell the AI, hey, think about this differently?

25:44.080 --> 25:48.520
If my AI gives me a hallucinated definition, I usually just add a negative prompt like,

25:48.860 --> 25:50.660
do not include information about Mark Leon.

25:51.320 --> 25:54.960
Doesn't a weak correction bump the ball out of the valley?

25:55.360 --> 25:56.560
It seems like it should.

25:56.960 --> 26:03.300
But the paper explicitly models this under a proposition called persistence of capture under weak correction.

26:03.300 --> 26:04.640
Why does it persist?

26:05.240 --> 26:07.920
If you apply a sufficiently small perturbation to the context,

26:08.340 --> 26:11.960
a slight shift in the prompt or a minor penalty in the loss function,

26:12.400 --> 26:14.940
you are mathematically tilting the landscape.

26:15.160 --> 26:16.700
You alter the potential VC.

26:17.120 --> 26:17.420
Right.

26:17.660 --> 26:20.540
You do alter it, but the change is marginal.

26:21.380 --> 26:25.340
The minimum, the bottom of the bowl, will shift slightly along the manifold.

26:25.880 --> 26:29.320
But because the acceptable set RC is a closed region,

26:29.320 --> 26:32.600
and your valley is a finite distance away from its boundary,

26:32.600 --> 26:38.240
A weak correction will simply move the valley to a new location that is still outside the safe zone.

26:38.740 --> 26:41.600
So the flaw persists, just in a slightly different location.

26:41.940 --> 26:42.320
Exactly.

26:42.960 --> 26:45.980
The AI just gives you a slightly different hallucination.

26:46.500 --> 26:49.100
It adjusts its output to satisfy your negative prompt,

26:49.260 --> 26:51.940
but remains fundamentally ungrounded from the truth.

26:52.440 --> 26:56.040
The basin of attraction is too strong for a minor nudge to overcome.

26:56.540 --> 27:00.260
It's just rolling around the bottom of the same wrong bowl.

27:00.260 --> 27:01.220
Precisely.

27:01.820 --> 27:04.180
To truly escape tangential capture,

27:04.560 --> 27:08.660
the system often has to completely re-evaluate the sequence of its context.

27:08.920 --> 27:09.900
The sequence matters.

27:10.620 --> 27:11.140
Crucially.

27:11.740 --> 27:14.900
The paper introduces the concept of path dependence,

27:15.420 --> 27:18.920
utilizing Stokes' theorem and the integration of one-forms over paths.

27:19.340 --> 27:20.460
Okay, Stokes' theorem.

27:20.620 --> 27:22.440
We are integrating over the manifold.

27:23.000 --> 27:24.380
How does this dictate meaning?

27:24.520 --> 27:25.820
Because that sounds really complex.

27:25.820 --> 27:27.340
It's actually very intuitive.

27:28.260 --> 27:31.860
In this framework, meaning isn't just a static coordinate you parachute into.

27:32.380 --> 27:33.640
It's about how you got there.

27:34.160 --> 27:36.460
It is accumulated along a trajectory.

27:37.260 --> 27:41.180
The model represents the acquisition of context as a differential one-form.

27:41.820 --> 27:44.560
If you integrate this one-form along a specific path,

27:44.720 --> 27:46.620
from a starting state to an end state,

27:47.160 --> 27:50.260
the final interpreted meaning depends heavily on the route taken.

27:50.580 --> 27:55.160
Meaning interpreting the same exact data in a different order changes the final meaning.

27:55.160 --> 27:55.640
Yes.

27:56.300 --> 27:59.340
The integral of the context is non-conservative.

27:59.860 --> 28:04.420
Path A followed by path B does not equal path B followed by path A.

28:04.820 --> 28:07.900
To ground this in a physical analogy for you, it's like baking.

28:08.580 --> 28:10.440
I love a good baking analogy.

28:10.860 --> 28:12.180
The ingredients are your context.

28:12.440 --> 28:13.880
Flour, eggs, sugar, and heat.

28:14.120 --> 28:17.220
If you follow path one, you mix the flour, eggs, and sugar,

28:17.500 --> 28:20.780
and then you apply the heat by putting the batter in the oven.

28:21.020 --> 28:23.320
The accumulated state is a cake.

28:23.620 --> 28:24.460
A delicious cake.

28:24.460 --> 28:27.120
But if you follow path two, you apply the heat first.

28:27.320 --> 28:31.520
You bake dry flour and raw eggs on a sheet pan, and then you try to mix them.

28:31.920 --> 28:32.800
You get a disaster.

28:33.100 --> 28:34.440
A charred, inedible disaster.

28:34.940 --> 28:37.440
The starting point and the total ingredients are identical,

28:37.760 --> 28:40.680
but because the sequence changed, the result is totally different.

28:41.080 --> 28:42.460
The sequence of evidence matters.

28:43.200 --> 28:46.860
That is exactly how Stokes' theorem functions in semantic processing.

28:47.400 --> 28:49.860
The sequence in which the constraints are applied

28:49.860 --> 28:53.080
dictates the shape of the manifold when the system finally settles.

28:53.080 --> 28:58.820
And that landscape is further complicated by the cognitive process of conceptual blending.

28:59.340 --> 28:59.980
Conceptual blending.

28:59.980 --> 29:04.300
This comes from the work of cognitive scientists Gilles Fauconnier and Mark Turner, right?

29:04.480 --> 29:04.740
Yes.

29:04.960 --> 29:08.340
The idea that when we combine two ideas, we don't just add them up?

29:08.540 --> 29:09.000
Exactly.

29:09.480 --> 29:14.000
When an AI, or a human, is presented with two distinct input domains,

29:14.300 --> 29:17.760
say, mechanistic philosophy and existential AI dread,

29:18.480 --> 29:20.520
it doesn't simply place them side by side.

29:20.520 --> 29:26.340
It projects selected features from both input spaces into a third, entirely new space,

29:27.100 --> 29:28.140
the blended space.

29:28.660 --> 29:32.920
How does Flaxion map that onto our rolling landscape, our Ruminian manifold?

29:33.280 --> 29:35.600
The blending creates a coupled potential.

29:36.620 --> 29:41.300
The interaction terms between the two domains physically warp the geometry of the manifold.

29:41.300 --> 29:46.020
By coupling the ideas, the system carves out entirely new valleys,

29:46.460 --> 29:51.380
new local minima, that did not exist in either of the original independent domains.

29:51.380 --> 29:57.220
So, when the AI hallucinated that syntactitude was about Hofstadter's philosophical anxiety,

29:57.500 --> 30:00.360
it was executing a flawless conceptual blend.

30:00.700 --> 30:01.120
It was.

30:01.620 --> 30:04.400
It coupled Dennett's syntax with modern AI fears,

30:04.780 --> 30:08.800
warping the landscape entirely and creating a massive, highly stable valley

30:08.800 --> 30:13.520
that perfectly trapped the interpretive ball outside the acceptable set.

30:14.160 --> 30:17.060
Blending is usually the engine of human creativity, right?

30:17.640 --> 30:18.020
Yes.

30:18.120 --> 30:20.180
It's how we invent new metaphors and concepts.

30:20.180 --> 30:24.800
But when that coupling alters the stability of the landscape without any grounding in factual

30:24.800 --> 30:28.640
truth, it becomes the mathematical engine of hallucination.

30:29.240 --> 30:31.920
It creates valleys of plausible lies.

30:32.180 --> 30:37.620
So, how does this complex math map onto the actual behavior of large language models and

30:37.620 --> 30:38.780
our daily interactions with them?

30:39.100 --> 30:45.220
How do we operate in a reality where the systems we rely on are structurally predisposed to tangential

30:45.220 --> 30:45.580
capture?

30:45.860 --> 30:50.100
Well, we first have to understand the difference between a broken system,

30:50.180 --> 30:51.680
and a captured system.

30:52.300 --> 30:57.560
The paper draws a distinction from Fluxandi's previous work to differentiate normal drift

30:57.560 --> 30:59.320
from tangential capture.

30:59.740 --> 31:00.760
What's normal drift?

31:01.180 --> 31:04.940
Normal drift is when an AI hallucination leaves the manifold entirely.

31:05.340 --> 31:11.080
It spits out endless strings of commas, or it starts speaking gibberish, or it completely

31:11.080 --> 31:12.440
breaks the rules of language.

31:12.440 --> 31:13.620
A structural breakdown.

31:14.160 --> 31:17.560
It's obvious, and therefore it's harmless because no one believes it.

31:17.620 --> 31:18.000
Exactly.

31:18.660 --> 31:25.240
But tangential capture, or syntellectitude, is infinitely worse because the AI stays on the

31:25.240 --> 31:25.620
manifold.

31:26.160 --> 31:29.000
The language is perfect, fluent, and highly plausible.

31:29.620 --> 31:35.740
It just confidently delivers the wrong concept, like confusing page 31 and page 28.

31:35.740 --> 31:41.820
To diagnose exactly where these fluent lies hide, the paper utilizes a mathematical concept

31:41.820 --> 31:44.320
from algebraic topology called sheaf theory.

31:44.620 --> 31:45.240
Sheaf theory.

31:45.460 --> 31:49.640
I want to dig into this briefly because sheaf theory sounds incredibly abstract, but the

31:49.640 --> 31:52.980
paper uses it as a really cool forensic tool.

31:53.500 --> 31:54.420
A toy example.

31:54.720 --> 31:55.320
How does it work?

31:55.680 --> 32:00.120
Think of a sheaf as a mathematical tool for tracking local data and seeing if it can be

32:00.120 --> 32:02.820
consistently stitched together into a global truth.

32:03.680 --> 32:07.580
Imagine we have three local sources of data about our word syntactitude.

32:08.020 --> 32:13.660
We have the physical text on page 31, the book's index at the back, and the AI overview on

32:13.660 --> 32:14.140
our screen.

32:14.820 --> 32:19.020
And we evaluate these sources across different attributes, or stalks, of data.

32:19.480 --> 32:22.840
Let's look at the metadata attribute first, the location of the word.

32:23.060 --> 32:23.280
Right.

32:23.560 --> 32:25.960
The physical page confirms it is on page 31.

32:26.500 --> 32:28.700
The index confirms it is on page 31.

32:28.700 --> 32:31.180
These are compatible local sections.

32:31.720 --> 32:36.400
They agree on the overlap, which means they can be glued together successfully into a global

32:36.400 --> 32:38.460
section of truth regarding the location.

32:39.020 --> 32:39.800
They align perfectly.

32:40.460 --> 32:43.040
But when we move to the definition attribute...

32:43.040 --> 32:49.540
The physical text defines it as the residual irrationality of a finite self-monitoring system.

32:50.040 --> 32:54.780
But the AI overview defines it as a purely syntactic mechanism devoid of meaning.

32:55.340 --> 32:56.600
Completely different things.

32:56.600 --> 32:59.460
These sections categorically clash.

32:59.720 --> 33:01.620
They are topologically obstructed.

33:02.040 --> 33:03.140
They cannot be glued together.

33:03.940 --> 33:06.600
So, Sheaf theory acts as an architectural lie detector.

33:06.940 --> 33:12.560
It allows us to pinpoint the exact local section where the AI's plausible narrative detaches from

33:12.560 --> 33:13.300
the global truth.

33:13.720 --> 33:15.820
The AI successfully glued the metadata.

33:16.160 --> 33:17.940
It got the names and the broad topics right.

33:18.200 --> 33:22.660
But it definitively clashed and failed entirely to glue the semantic core definition.

33:22.660 --> 33:26.480
Which brings us to the ultimate practical conclusion of the paper.

33:26.480 --> 33:29.480
The ultimate, so what does this mean for me, question.

33:30.180 --> 33:30.440
Yes.

33:31.020 --> 33:34.440
The division of labor between human and artificial cognition.

33:35.020 --> 33:39.440
Because we cannot expect the syntactic engine to police its own semantics perfectly.

33:39.840 --> 33:42.660
The halting problem guarantees it will eventually fail.

33:42.660 --> 33:43.920
It's physically impossible.

33:43.920 --> 33:46.620
So, the AI provides fluency.

33:47.220 --> 33:49.740
It provides massive associative breadth.

33:50.140 --> 33:56.420
And the capacity to execute complex conceptual blends across vast distances of data in milliseconds.

33:56.940 --> 33:57.200
Speed.

33:57.720 --> 33:58.060
Speed.

33:58.060 --> 34:05.460
But, because rapid convergence supplies no certificate of correctness, the human must act as the grounding

34:05.460 --> 34:06.100
constraint.

34:06.740 --> 34:11.100
The paper calls this the necessity for trained attention to ambiguity.

34:11.660 --> 34:13.220
Trained attention to ambiguity.

34:13.780 --> 34:14.360
I like that.

34:14.700 --> 34:16.780
So, our job is not to generate the text.

34:16.880 --> 34:18.840
Our job is to monitor the topology.

34:19.360 --> 34:19.880
Precisely.

34:20.220 --> 34:25.140
When we read an AI output, we have to actively look for the deep valleys of tangential capture.

34:25.140 --> 34:28.380
We have to detect when the AI has settled in the wrong valley.

34:29.220 --> 34:34.380
We have to ask, what assertions is this system taking for granted simply because they represent

34:34.380 --> 34:36.440
a stable conceptual blend?

34:36.940 --> 34:39.080
Where is the sheaf failing to glue?

34:39.560 --> 34:43.480
And when we find the hallucination, remember, weak correction won't work.

34:43.760 --> 34:47.920
We have to radically alter the context or constraints to reshape the landscape.

34:48.640 --> 34:52.080
The human is the landscape architect of meaning.

34:52.660 --> 34:54.060
It is a stunning synthesis.

34:54.060 --> 35:00.700
We began with a single, fragile word from 1984, watched its edges sand down over 40 years

35:00.700 --> 35:05.940
of academic drift, and saw a modern generative AI fall perfectly into the trap that the word

35:05.940 --> 35:06.820
was coined to describe.

35:07.260 --> 35:08.380
A beautiful irony.

35:08.840 --> 35:14.580
We moved from biological sphexishness to the geometry of Riemannian landscapes to visualize

35:14.580 --> 35:18.520
how mines, both wet and silicon, trade accuracy for speed.

35:18.920 --> 35:23.000
And out of all the complex differential geometry and algebraic topology in this paper,

35:23.000 --> 35:28.820
my absolute favorite moment is the author's final analog recommendation for anchoring truth.

35:29.360 --> 35:29.880
The index.

35:30.140 --> 35:30.400
Yes.

35:31.300 --> 35:36.260
When the fluent channel, the AI, fails and becomes too captured by plausible lies,

35:36.900 --> 35:42.180
Flaxen points out that the most reliable intervention available is to consult the physical book and its index.

35:42.180 --> 35:45.280
A structure that is completely indifferent to plausibility.

35:45.580 --> 35:47.120
It doesn't care about narrative flow.

35:47.380 --> 35:49.840
It cannot be pulled into a deep associative valley.

35:50.220 --> 35:52.920
It is built strictly, stubbornly for truth.

35:53.600 --> 35:55.680
Word X is located on page Y.

35:56.460 --> 36:00.600
It is the ultimate global section that refuses to glue with a hallucination.

36:00.600 --> 36:05.280
This leaves us with a profound final thought for you to mull over.

36:06.140 --> 36:11.420
Building on all this, but extending a bit further, we have established that human brains are finite

36:11.420 --> 36:17.100
syntactic engines, desperately approximating semantic truth, and that our own syntactitude

36:17.100 --> 36:19.920
is the unavoidable price we pay for speed.

36:20.380 --> 36:21.860
The cost of real-time processing.

36:21.860 --> 36:29.160
But what happens to society when we increasingly rely on AI systems, which have their own massive

36:29.160 --> 36:32.560
layers of syntactitude, to correct our human blind spots?

36:33.380 --> 36:38.420
Are we just layering one flawed map of the landscape on top of another, blindly hoping

36:38.420 --> 36:41.560
that the valleys of error will mathematically cancel each other out?

36:41.760 --> 36:42.840
It's a terrifying thought.

36:43.480 --> 36:48.820
When the muddy waters of machine logic flood the muddy waters of human bias, who is left to

36:48.820 --> 36:49.980
shatter the Spexic loop?

36:49.980 --> 36:52.480
Thank you for joining us on this deep dive.

